## Acceptance Criteria (EARS Format)

<!--
Use EARS (Easy Approach to Requirements Syntax) for all acceptance criteria.
EARS makes criteria directly testable and eliminates ambiguity.

Five EARS patterns:

1. UBIQUITOUS (always active):
   "THE SYSTEM SHALL {behavior}"
   Example: "The system shall display timestamps in the user's local timezone."

2. EVENT-DRIVEN (triggered by an event):
   "WHEN {event} THE SYSTEM SHALL {behavior}"
   Example: "When the user clicks Submit, the system shall validate all required fields."

3. STATE-DRIVEN (depends on system state):
   "WHILE {state} THE SYSTEM SHALL {behavior}"
   Example: "While the user is unauthenticated, the system shall redirect all protected routes to /login."

4. UNWANTED (handles failure conditions):
   "IF {condition} THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL {behavior}"
   Example: "If the API returns a 429 status, then the system shall retry with exponential backoff up to 3 times."

5. OPTIONAL (depends on a feature flag or config):
   "WHERE {condition} THE SYSTEM SHALL {behavior}"
   Example: "Where dark mode is enabled, the system shall apply the dark color palette to all components."

Guidelines:
  - Each criterion maps to exactly one test case
  - Use SHALL (not should, must, or will) for mandatory behavior
  - Include the trigger condition (WHEN/WHILE/IF/WHERE) for context-dependent behavior
  - Keep each criterion to a single sentence
-->

| # | Criterion |
|---|-----------|
| 1 | WHEN {event} THE SYSTEM SHALL {behavior} |
| 2 | IF {condition} THEN THE SYSTEM SHALL {behavior} |
| 3 | THE SYSTEM SHALL {behavior} |

